WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.593
All track long, one picture kept coming back.

2
00:00:05.593 --> 00:00:09.128
The recipe card decides, and the cook does the errands.

3
00:00:09.128 --> 00:00:11.121
The card never lights the oven.

4
00:00:11.121 --> 00:00:13.628
And the cook never makes up the recipe.

5
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:17.693
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:20.273
Everyone says, keep your functions pure.

7
00:00:20.273 --> 00:00:24.465
But my program has to read the clock, send orders and save files.

8
00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:26.464
So where does the pure part go?

9
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:29.486
Here's a job from the recipe app.

10
00:00:29.486 --> 00:00:32.760
Every morning, LegacyShoppingJob runs one big loop.

11
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.778
It reads the clock and loads the shopping list.

12
00:00:35.778 --> 00:00:44.639
It skips crossed off items, holds back items it's too early to order, adds up a total per shop, and works out each shop's member discount.

13
00:00:44.639 --> 00:00:50.611
Then it sends the orders, writes each one into the household budget book, and saves the book.

14
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:51.389
It works.

15
00:00:51.389 --> 00:00:54.046
But say you want to test one discount rule.

16
00:00:54.046 --> 00:00:59.546
You need a fake store, a fake shop, a fixed clock, and checks on what the fakes recorded.

17
00:00:59.546 --> 00:01:03.809
That's a whole kitchen of props, to taste one spoonful of arithmetic.

18
00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:07.194
So let's split it the way the kitchen does.

19
00:01:07.194 --> 00:01:15.238
The card does the deciding: plain values go in, today's date included, and out comes a plan, a value that says what should happen.

20
00:01:15.238 --> 00:01:21.178
The cook is everything that touches the world: the clock, the list, the orders, the budget book.

21
00:01:21.178 --> 00:01:23.035
And the cook follows the plan.

22
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:27.710
This shape has a name: functional core, imperative shell.

23
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.271
The functional core is a pure function.

24
00:01:30.271 --> 00:01:32.307
For our job, it's planShopping.

25
00:01:32.307 --> 00:01:36.772
It takes the items, the shops and today, and returns a ShoppingPlan.

26
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.618
Everything from this track fits inside.

27
00:01:39.618 --> 00:01:43.108
filterNot, partition, groupBy and sumOf do the work.

28
00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:45.121
Data classes of vals carry it.

29
00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:54.115
And expected outcomes, like an item from a shop the app doesn't know, come back as values in the plan, not as exceptions or log lines.

30
00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:57.932
The imperative shell is the thin outer part.

31
00:01:57.932 --> 00:02:02.741
It's the only code that touches the world, and it has almost no branches.

32
00:02:02.741 --> 00:02:04.322
Read today and the list.

33
00:02:04.322 --> 00:02:06.167
Decide, by calling the core.

34
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.933
Act: send each order, and save the budget.

35
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:13.184
Let's shrink it to something we can run: the fridge.

36
00:02:13.184 --> 00:02:16.183
Leftovers older than three days get thrown out.

37
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:17.778
decideFridge is the core.

38
00:02:17.778 --> 00:02:21.733
It takes the leftovers and the time now, both as plain values.

39
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:26.007
Now is the 10th of March, at 9 in the morning.

40
00:02:26.007 --> 00:02:28.923
The soup, l1, was cooked 4 days before.

41
00:02:28.923 --> 00:02:31.166
The rice, l2, 30 hours before.

42
00:02:31.166 --> 00:02:33.111
What does the core decide?

43
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.884
l1 goes: ThrowOut.

44
00:02:37.884 --> 00:02:40.956
l2 stays: Keep, good for PT42H.

45
00:02:40.956 --> 00:02:47.299
That's how Java writes 42 hours: three days, 72 hours, minus 30.

46
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.429
And the shell?

47
00:02:48.429 --> 00:02:54.799
It loads the leftovers, reads the clock, calls the core, and sends a reminder for each ThrowOut.

48
00:02:54.799 --> 00:03:00.107
With a fixed clock and a fake phone, it prints: reminder, throw out leftover l1.

49
00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:03.374
Now push the time one day later, to the 11th.

50
00:03:03.374 --> 00:03:04.929
Which decision changes?

51
00:03:04.929 --> 00:03:07.564
Try to answer without running anything.

52
00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:11.690
Only the hours.

53
00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:16.394
l2 is still kept, now with PT18H left, and l1 goes as before.

54
00:03:16.394 --> 00:03:21.561
You can check that from the core alone, with no clock and no phone.

55
00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:24.886
We just checked a fridge without opening the fridge.

56
00:03:24.886 --> 00:03:30.164
So the point really is: when the time is a parameter, a decision is tested with a value.

57
00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:32.552
That changes the tests.

58
00:03:32.552 --> 00:03:38.754
The core gets many small tests: build a list, call the function, compare the result with equals.

59
00:03:38.754 --> 00:03:41.209
No fakes, no clock, milliseconds each.

60
00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:45.408
And the shell gets one test with fakes, just to check the wiring.

61
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:48.854
The kitchen picture breaks in one place.

62
00:03:48.854 --> 00:03:52.068
A real cook can improvise when something looks off.

63
00:03:52.068 --> 00:03:53.265
The shell must not.

64
00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:58.307
Any rule that slips into the shell is a rule you can only test with fakes again.

65
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:00.541
And the honest limit.

66
00:04:00.541 --> 00:04:02.782
Neither compiler checks any of this.

67
00:04:02.782 --> 00:04:06.268
Kotlin has no keyword for pure, or for deeply immutable.

68
00:04:06.268 --> 00:04:12.867
And a read-only List can still change underneath the core, if the caller keeps the mutable list behind it.

69
00:04:12.867 --> 00:04:15.917
So the shell copies at the boundary, with toList.

70
00:04:15.917 --> 00:04:19.092
The shell stays thin only because you keep it thin.

71
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.973
So, where does the pure part go?

72
00:04:21.973 --> 00:04:22.837
In the middle.

73
00:04:22.837 --> 00:04:25.735
Decide in the core, with every input passed in.

74
00:04:25.735 --> 00:04:29.435
Let the shell read, call the core and act, and nothing more.

75
00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:31.164
Quick check.

76
00:04:31.164 --> 00:04:36.744
A function reads LocalDate.now, and decides whether a bag of flour is past its date.

77
00:04:36.744 --> 00:04:39.667
How do you move that decision into the core?

78
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:44.484
Pass today in as a parameter.

79
00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:46.143
The shell reads the clock.

80
00:04:46.143 --> 00:04:50.483
The core only compares two dates, and you test it with plain values.
